Today at Ignite, we’re announcing the new Microsoft Copilot SDK (previously named [GitHub Copilot SDK](https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-28-github-copilot-sdk-is-becoming-microsoft-copilot-sdk/)). It gives you direct, programmatic access to the [Microsoft Copilot harness](https://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2026/10/28/introducing-the-microsoft-copilot-harness-unlocking-the-potential-of-truly-multi-model-agentic-work/) powering products across our entire portfolio for coding, non-coding, and creative apps, platforms, and services. It can be used to build agents that run anywhere and can use any inference.

![Image 1](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/fig-stack-1024x609.png)
## Sessions in isolation

Since the [Copilot SDK](https://github.blog/changelog/2026-06-02-copilot-sdk-is-now-generally-available/) launch earlier this year, many teams across GitHub and Microsoft have adopted the runtime as a shared platform for Copilot agents across our services. For many use cases, hosting agents in a single container was and still is the right answer for their architectures. Most teams within Microsoft give every session its own sandbox, so data stays inside a managed boundary. If an agent compiles code, runs untrusted scripts, or needs a custom image, a hard container boundary remains an essential part of their infrastructure.

The problem was that, before today, the container was the only option, so its tradeoffs leaked into every implementation. Every session used a container whether it needed one or not. This meant that the first turn had to wait on infrastructure, cost scaled rapidly with sessions, and the session’s identity was the same as the process running it.

![Image 2: From one container per session to one runtime, many sessions.](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/01-fig-containers-1024x570.png)

From one container per session to one runtime, many sessions.

## Virtualizing an agent’s environment

The new Microsoft Copilot SDK removes the assumption that the runtime owns the machine it runs on. The shell, disk and the identity of whoever started the runtime were replaced with new abstraction layers that give developers the control they need to design and deploy performant agents from simple chat assistants to complex multi-agent software factories.

![Image 3](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/02-fig-abstractions.png)
### A shared, multi-tenanted runtime

The first big change in 2.0 is a **runtime that can host many sessions within a single process**. Each session has its own state, credentials, tools, files, and history. No session can see another. Sessions become cheap and highly disposable. Creating one doesn’t need to start a container, a process, or a VM, which cuts both time to first token and operating cost. When users ask a simple question, the runtime can respond immediately with performant inference.

That only works if the runtime itself is lean, so we rebuilt it in Rust. On the same warmed workload, creating a client and a session and completing one turn went from 5.25 seconds on the TypeScript CLI to 55.3 milliseconds with the Rust core in-process. Scalability improved as well: 10 clients now use 126 MB of memory instead of 1,383 MB, and throughput rose from 7.55 to 120 sessions per second.

![Image 4: The multi-tenant runtime starts with a much lighter core.](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/03-fig-rust-core-1024x365.png)

The multi-tenant runtime starts with a much lighter core.

From the SDK, a shared runtime is just a connection. Many application instances can point at the same runtime service using our new empty mode:

```
import { CopilotClient } from "@microsoft/copilot-sdk"; 

const client = new CopilotClient({ 
  mode: "empty", // start from nothing: no ambient shell, files, or CLI defaults 
});
```

### Identities scoped to sessions, not processes

Before 2.0, the runtime acted under the identity that instantiated it. Now, **each session can run independently as the identity of the user who requested it.** You pass that user’s credential when you create the session, and every model call and tool call the session makes is attributed to that user, not to your server.

```
const session = await client.createSession({ 
  gitHubToken: user.githubToken, // or gitHubTokenProvider for short-lived, rotating tokens 
});
```

For production services, a `gitHubTokenProvider` callback lets the runtime request a fresh token per session as tokens expire, so you never store long-lived credentials.

### Host-owned tools and policies

A shared runtime is only safe if no session can reach the machine it runs on. `mode: "empty"` starts every session with minimal ambient capabilities: no host shell, no host file system, no default tools. **You add back exactly what a session should have**, and the tools you register run in your process, against your data, under your rules.

```
import { defineTool } from "@microsoft/copilot-sdk"; 

const lookupOrder = defineTool("lookup_order", { 
  description: "Look up one of the signed-in customer's orders.", 
  parameters: { 
    type: "object", 
    properties: { orderId: { type: "string" } }, 
    required: ["orderId"], 
  }, 
  handler: async ({ orderId }) => orders.getForCustomer(user.id, orderId), 
}); 

const session = await client.createSession({ 
  gitHubToken: user.githubToken, 
  mode: "empty", 
  tools: [lookupOrder], 
  availableTools: ["custom:lookup_order"], // an explicit allow-list per session 
});
```

### Long lasting, durable and resumable sessions

In a container-per-session world, the session lived as long as the container. With the new runtime, **a session is durable and outlives any process or client connected to it.**

Two abstractions help make that work.

The first is the session journal. As a conversation progresses, the runtime appends everything it knows about the session, such as messages, tool calls, and their results, plans, and checkpoints, to an event log. Any runtime that can read that log can resume the session with its full history, whether that’s the same process after a restart, another instance of your service, or a different machine.

The second is sessionFs, which decides where that log lives. By default, the runtime keeps session state on its own disk. With sessionFs, your application supplies the file system instead, so session state and session-scoped files can live in object storage, a database, or tenant-scoped paths you control. Local disk becomes disposable:

```
const client = new CopilotClient({ 
  mode: "empty", 
  sessionFs: { 
    initialCwd: "/work", 
    sessionStatePath: "/state", 
    conventions: "posix", 
  }, 
}); 

const session = await client.createSession({ 
  sessionId: "user-123-task-456", 
  // Your provider decides where this session's journal and files live 
  createSessionFsProvider: (s) => tenantStorage(user.tenantId, s.sessionId), 
});
```

Any instance of your service can then pick the session back up by its ID, once you’ve checked that the caller owns it:

```
// Later, from any instance of your service: 
const session = await client.resumeSession("user-123-task-456", { 
  createSessionFsProvider: (s) => tenantStorage(user.tenantId, s.sessionId), 
}); 
await session.sendAndWait({ prompt: "Where did we land on the Q3 numbers?" });
```

Because the session state lives outside any one process, clients can come and go. A web app can disconnect while a long task keeps running. A teammate can attach GitHub Copilot CLI to the same session to inspect or steer it. A webhook can pick a conversation back up hours later. Idle sandboxes can stop to save cost and resume, with disks intact, on the next message.

This is the same foundation as session continuity in GitHub Copilot: start a session on one surface and pick it up on another with its history intact. When you need one _live_ session across many clients at once, the SDK can run behind an [Agent Host Protocol (AHP) host](https://aka.ms/ahp-1.0). AHP is a separate, open protocol that turns an agent session into a shared, synchronized resource that multiple clients can attach to. VS Code’s agent host works this way, built on the Copilot SDK.

### Native in-process embedding

We want the SDK to feel like a standard library for building agents, so we exposed a C ABI that’s available in every programming language and switched the runtime to run in-process by default. Whether it runs in your process, as a child process, or as a shared service, your agent code doesn’t change.

```
const client = new CopilotClient({ 
  connection: RuntimeConnection.forInProcess(), 
});
```

## Six agentic architectures you can build on one runtime

The Microsoft Copilot SDK doesn’t choose an architecture for you; it gives you a portable runtime with the multi-tenant capabilities to build the one your product needs—local or in the cloud. That lets you make two decisions separately: **where the agent thinks** (the model loop) and **where it acts** (shell and file work).

![Image 5: Where the agent thinks, and where it acts.](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/04-fig-six-1024x762.png)

Where the agent thinks, and where it acts.

### Many sessions in a shared runtime

The simplest architecture you can build with our new runtime puts many sessions on one shared process. There’s no sandbox: tools are your app’s APIs, MCP servers, or web calls, and they run under each user’s identity. There’s no container start before the first turn, and each additional session costs very little.

![Image 6: Every new user gets a session, not a container.](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/05-fig-shared-1.gif)

Every new user gets a session, not a container.

With no shell or access to a local file system, isolation is enforced by the application rather than a container boundary. Optimal for some assistants, but largely unhelpful for coding scenarios or complex knowledge work by itself.

### Start shared, move when needed

Most conversations start light and stay light. Some don’t. In a promotable architecture, every session **starts on the shared runtime, and your app moves a session into its own sandbox** when the work needs a shell or files. The session keeps the same ID, the same conversation, and the same tools, and your app holds the same session object before and after.

![Image 7: A session starts on the shared runtime and moves into its own sandbox only when the work needs one.](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-fig-promotable.gif)

A session starts on the shared runtime and moves into its own sandbox only when the work needs one.

The cost is a single wait while the sandbox starts, and the move is one-way. The gain is that you pay for containers only for the sessions that need them, at the moment they need them.

### Shared agent loop with sandboxed environments

Sometimes you want isolated execution without running a whole agent in every sandbox. To allow the runtime to directly drive a container, **the model loop stays on the shared runtime and drives a sandbox environment** with a shell and files but no agent. Host-owned tools make it work: the session’s `bash`, `view`, and `edit` tools are replaced with host implementations that run each command in the sandbox.

![Image 8: The model loop stays on the shared runtime; only its commands run in the sandbox](https://commandline.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/07-fig-direct.gif)

The model loop stays on the shared runtime; only its commands run in the sandbox.

